tively. B.B. and T.S. are supported by the ÚNKP-22-4 and ÚNKP-22-5 New National Excellence Programs of the Ministry for Culture and Innovation from the source of the NRDI Fund, Hungary. T.S. is also supported by the János Bolyai Research Scholarship of the Hungarian Academy of Sciences. The research of J.C.W. and J.V. is supported by NSF grant No. AST-1813825. K.M. acknowledges support from the Japan Society for the Promotion of Science (JSPS) KAKENHI grant No. JP20H00174, and the JSPS Open Partnership Bilateral Joint Research Project (grant No. JPJSBP120209937). Time-domain research by D.J.S. and the University of Arizona team is supported by NSF grant Nos. AST-1821987, 1813466, 1908972, and 2108032, and by the Heising-Simons Foundation under grant No. 2020–1864. M.R.S. is supported by an STScI Postdoctoral Fellowship. G.D. acknowledges H2020 European Research Council grant No. 758638. L.A.K. acknowledges support by a NASA FINESST fellowship grant No. 80NSSC22K1599. C.L. is supported by a National Science Foundation (NSF) Graduate Research Fellowship under grant No. DGE-2233066. This work makes use of data from the Las Cumbres Observatory global network of telescopes. The LCO group is supported by NSF grant Nos. AST-1911151 and AST-1911225. This work also makes use of data gathered with the 6.5 m Magellan telescopes at Las Campanas Observatory, Chile. This publication was made possible through the support of an LSSTC Catalyst Fellowship to K.A.B., funded through grant No. 62192 from the John Templeton Foundation to the LSST Corporation. The opinions expressed in this publication are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect the views of LSSTC or the John Templeton Foundation. L.G. acknowledges financial support from the Spanish Ministerio de Ciencia e Innovación (MCIN), the Agencia Estatal de Investigación (AEI; doi: 10.13039/501100011033 ), and the European Social Fund (ESF) “Investing in your future” under the 2019 Ramón y Cajal program RYC2019-027683-I and the PID2020-115253GA-I00 HOSTFLOWS project, from Centro Superior de Investigaciones Científicas (CSIC) under the PIE project 20215AT016, and the program Unidad de Excelencia María de Maeztu CEX2020-001058-M. Some of the data presented herein were obtained at the W. M. Keck Observatory, which is operated as a scientific partnership among the California Institute of Technology, the University of California, and NASA. The Observatory was made possible by the generous financial support of the W. M. Keck Foundation. The authors wish to recognize and acknowledge the very significant cultural role and reverence that the summit of Maunakea has always had within the indigenous Hawaiian community. We are most fortunate to have the opportunity to conduct observations from this mountain. J.P.H. acknowledges support from the George A. and Margaret M. Downsbrough bequest. The Aarhus supernova group is funded by a project 2 grant (doi: 10.46540/2032-00022B ) from the Independent Research Fund Denmark (IRFD). The data were obtained from the Mikulski Archive for Space Telescopes at the Space Telescope Science Institute (STScI), which is operated by the Association of Universities for Research in Astronomy (AURA), Inc., under National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) contract NAS 5-03127 for JWST. Support for this program at Rutgers University was provided by NASA through grant No. JWST-GO-02072.001."],"id":"S01-000.00-717310","identifier":[{"id":"85182988590","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-218346b7-6dcb-4eb6-9dea-01d2238a229d"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-ADE33D3BFF188B936901C9AD96D4981F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ad0975","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"space telescope science institute","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Space Telescope Science Institute","used":1},{"affn