{"abstract":"Reclaiming Technology: A poetic-scientific vocabulary aims to evoke ways of thinking about, and intervening in, technology worlds through a series of blended writing styles. Consisting of thirty-five short essays, contributors were asked to problematize some aspect of their research with technology – broadly conceived as artifact, process, craft, infrastructure, or concept – without being constrained by the more traditional repertoires of academic publishing. The result is a set of critical and experimental texts whose vocabulary speaks to the poetic, affective, and mundane modes through which technology can inspire and make anew. The ambition to reclaim stems from a collective desire to shift the grounds upon which exuberant technological claims making occurs. The book’s poetic-scientific approach is an effort at rendering technology otherwise so as to enable the emergence of alternate claims-making pathways towards more just and dignified socio-technical futures. The book should appeal to those who are curious about the technology-society nexus in more general terms. More particular audiences include anthropology and STS scholars, students (at all levels) interested in methods and writing, and practitioners looking for conceptual resources to think about technology differently.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Maguire, James","Winthereik, Brit Ross"],"cuid":["S02-092.31-681466"],"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Technology poetic reclaiming"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reclaiming technology a poetic scientific vocabularly","src":["dtu","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101322945/Reclaiming_Technology_-_A_poetic-scientific_vocabulary_ebook-pdf_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-092.31-681466","ids":["dtu-31657034-cba8-4978-9016-2cca16ba0d56","itu-ff923926-c3c5-4b3d-9f25-b9502507bf86"],"keyword":["Technology, poetic, reclaiming"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-2-604269D9A99DF002A3EBD84EDB8C65EB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101322945/Reclaiming_Technology_-_A_poetic-scientific_vocabulary_ebook-pdf_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://cadb.dk/product/reclaiming-technology-a-poetic-scientific-vocabulary-kopier/?lang=en","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"rem","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jyU0CBs3MH97Ct2T051sd5hFiheMhfQY/view","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Technology, Management and Economics","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Technology, Management and Economics, Technology and Business Studies","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Technology, Management and Economics, Technology and Business Studies, Responsible Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"James","key":"maguirepau","last":"Maguire","locid":"2efbc294-1ce4-4148-a403-93fe6450d879","name":"Maguire, James","orcid":"0000-0001-5081-1811","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Brit Ross","key":"winthereikped","last":"Winthereik","locid":"16cd0449-773d-4f11-a3ef-7f9143ab83e8","name":"Winthereik, Brit Ross","orcid":"0000-0002-1837-2385","rank":2,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9788799873364"],"isbn":["9788799873364"],"pages":"168","place":"Copenhagen","publisher":"Ctrl+Alt+Delete Books","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-31657034-cba8-4978-9016-2cca16ba0d56","itu-ff923926-c3c5-4b3d-9f25-b9502507bf86"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Reclaiming Technology : a poetic-scientific vocabularly","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-092.31-681466","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"We present JWST near-infrared (NIR) and mid-infrared (MIR) spectroscopic observations of the nearby normal Type Ia supernova (SN) SN 2021aefx in the nebular phase at +255 days past maximum light. Our Near Infrared Spectrograph (NIRSpec) and Mid Infrared Instrument observations, combined with ground-based optical data from the South African Large Telescope, constitute the first complete optical+NIR+MIR nebular SN Ia spectrum covering 0.3-14 μm. This spectrum unveils the previously unobserved 2.5−5 μm region, revealing strong nebular iron and stable nickel emission, indicative of high-density burning that can constrain the progenitor mass. The data show a significant improvement in sensitivity and resolution compared to previous Spitzer MIR data. We identify numerous NIR and MIR nebular emission lines from iron-group elements as well as lines from the intermediate-mass element argon. The argon lines extend to higher velocities than the iron-group elements, suggesting stratified ejecta that are a hallmark of delayed-detonation or double-detonation SN Ia models. We present fits to simple geometric line profiles to features beyond 1.2 μm and find that most lines are consistent with Gaussian or spherical emission distributions, while the [Ar iii] 8.99 μm line has a distinctively flat-topped profile indicating a thick spherical shell of emission. Using our line profile fits, we investigate the emissivity structure of SN 2021aefx and measure kinematic properties. Continued observations of SN 2021aefx and other SNe Ia with JWST will be transformative to the study of SN Ia composition, ionization structure, density, and temperature, and will provide important constraints on SN Ia progenitor and explosion models.","author_count":50,"contributors":["Kwok, Lindsey A.","Jha, Saurabh W.","Temim, Tea","Fox, Ori D.","Larison, Conor","Camacho-Neves, Yssavo","Brenner Newman, Max J.","Pierel, Justin D.R.","Foley, Ryan J.","Andrews, Jennifer E.","Badenes, Carles","Barna, Ba