on-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Edinburgh","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"James","key":"maguireped","last":"Maguire","locid":"2efbc294-1ce4-4148-a403-93fe6450d879","name":"Maguire, James","orcid":"0000-0001-5081-1811","rank":1,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Laura","key":"wattsped","last":"Watts","name":"Watts, Laura","rank":2,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Brit Ross","key":"winthereikped","last":"Winthereik","name":"Winthereik, Brit Ross","rank":3,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.28938/9781912729098","eisbn":["9781912729098"],"isbn":["9781912729081"],"pages":"217","place":"Manchester","publisher":"Mattering Press","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7180c79e-dfc6-451f-bc79-c5035f54e303"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Energy Worlds in Experiment","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382950","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This paper explores an informal acoustic method developed by a group of industrial geologists working in geothermal energy landscapes in the southwest of Iceland. Through a series of ethnographic descriptions, this paper renders the work these geologists carry out in sonic terms, emphasizing how they use their bodies as sonic detectors in the production of geological evidence. Sound, the paper argues, is what allows geologists to make the intractable problem of volcanic cooling doable. It does this by differentiating two forms of evidence. Primary evidence, which ends up as data in geological reports, and secondary sonic evidence, which is what establishes that this primary evidence is, in fact, evidence. The paper introduces the concept data echoes as a way to think about how sound articulates between these evidential protocols. As echo, sound works as an outside, which, while remaining external to official protocols of knowledge production, nevertheless helps to constitute distinctions that are meaningful to the production of those categories. As data echoes through the various moments of data capture, analysis, and model building, sound’s temporal form helps to predict the time frame of volcanic cooling, as it affects both the immediate energy production scenarios and the long duree of volcanic time.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Maguire, James"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383241"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Evidence","Sound","acoustic methods"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"data echoes sound evidence and acoustic methods in energy landscapes","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/01622439211034575","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383241","identifier":[{"id":"85111163463","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8628e2fa-03f2-4e5e-9d77-f641538a131e"],"keyword":["acoustic methods","evidence","sound"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B28E1DE5209A09BFD7D89756AE4B8A94"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/01622439211034575","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86138614/Maguire_Data_Echoes_pre_print.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"James","key":"maguirepau","last":"Maguire","locid":"2efbc294-1ce4-4148-a403-93fe6450d879","name":"Maguire, James","orcid":"0000-0001-5081-1811","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/01622439211034575","issn":["01622439","15528251"],"issn_org":["01622439"],"journal":"Science, Technology & Human Values","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Science, Technology & Human Values"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85111163463"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8628e2fa-03f2-4e5e-9d77-f641538a131e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Data Echoes: Sound, Evidence, and Acoustic Methods in Energy Landscapes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383241","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: Domestic dogs represent a translational animal model to study naturally occurring human disease. Proteomics has emerged as a promising tool for characterizing human platelet pathophysiology; thus a detailed characterization of the core canine activated platelet secretome (CAPS) will enhance utilization of the canine model. The objectives of this study were development of a robust, high throughput, label-free approach for proteomic identification and quantification of the canine platelet (i) thrombin releasate proteins, and (ii) the protein subgroup that constitutes CAPS. Methods: Platelets were isolated from 10 healthy dogs and stimulated with 50 nmol/L of γ-thrombin or saline. Proteins were in-solution trypsin-digested and analyzed by nano–liquid chromatography–tandem spectrometry. Core releasate proteins were defined as those present in 10 of 10 dogs, and CAPS defined as releasate proteins with a significantly higher abundance in stimulated versus saline controls (corrected P <.05). Results: A total of 2865 proteins were identified; 1126 releasate proteins were present in all dogs, 650 were defined as CAPS. Among the differences from human platelets were a canine lack of platelet factor 4 and vascular endothelial growth factor C, and a 10- to 20-fold lower concentration of proteins such as haptoglobin, alpha-2 macroglobulin, von Willebrand factor, and amyloid-beta A4. Twenty-eight CAPS proteins, including cytokines, adhesion molecules, granule proteins, and calcium regulatory proteins have not previously been attributed to human platelets. Conclusions: CAPS proteins represent a robust characterization of a large animal platelet secretome and a novel tool to model platelet physiology, pathophysiology, and to identify translational biomarkers of platelet-mediated disease.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Cremer, Signe Emilie","Catalfamo, James L.","Goggs, Robert","Seemann, Ernst Stefan","Kristensen, Annemarie Thuri","Szklanna, Paulina B.","Maguire, Patricia B.","Brooks, Marjory B."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-446049"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Ireland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Cornell University","University College Dublin"],"keyword":["Dog","Platelet","Proteomics","Secretion"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the canine activated platelet secretome caps a translational model of thrombin evoked platelet activation response","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/rth2.12450","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-446049","identifier":[{"id":"PMC7845059","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"33537530","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85097011810","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-57fd9ad1-47fb-42c8-890b-e1470e3cde70"],"keyword":["dog","platelet","proteomics","releasate","secretion"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3D76E23435D55AC9B86D4B0544AD0AA1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/276956880/rth2.12450.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/rth2.12450","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Facult